WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.032 --> 00:00:04.921
The cell was small; he could cover it in three paces.

2
00:00:04.922 --> 00:00:08.580
His outstretched hands could touch opposing walls.

3
00:00:08.581 --> 00:00:13.743
His bed was a mat on the floor, and his toilet was a bucket.

4
00:00:13.744 --> 00:00:23.561
The man who had topped the most-wanted list of the South African police was finally confined; his name was Nelson Mandela.

5
00:00:23.562 --> 00:00:32.618
He was in prison for daring to demand basic human rights and citizenship for the majority black population of South Africa.

6
00:00:32.619 --> 00:00:42.511
Before the police finally caught him, Mandela successfully eluded authorities by moving from one hideout to another and using disguises.

7
00:00:42.512 --> 00:00:52.542
Mandela once posed as a garage worker and wheeled a spare tire right down the main street of Johannesburg, the largest city in South Africa.

8
00:00:52.543 --> 00:00:58.742
But in 1962, Mandela was finally stopped at a police roadblock.

9
00:00:58.743 --> 00:01:06.368
Nelson Rolihlahla Mandela was born in South Africa on July 18, 1918.

10
00:01:06.369 --> 00:01:22.456
Because his father was a chief of the Thembu tribe, Mandela was exposed early on to the tribal elders’ stories about his people’s struggle against colonialism, the domination of native peoples around the world by European nations.

11
00:01:22.457 --> 00:01:32.783
Whites were not native to South Africa, and since their arrival in 1647, they had gradually pushed native Africans from their tribal lands.

12
00:01:32.784 --> 00:01:42.269
A major characteristic of European colonialism was the passage of laws, from the moment of settlement, which favored the white colonists.

13
00:01:42.270 --> 00:01:53.216
By 1948, a rigid set of laws had developed that deprived black Africans of most of their human rights and segregated them from white society.

14
00:01:53.217 --> 00:02:07.900
This legal segregation, called "apartheid," separated South Africans into three racial groups&#8212;whites, blacks, and "coloreds" (Africans of mixed race, Indians, and Asians).

15
00:02:07.901 --> 00:02:26.516
Under apartheid, the members of these groups could not intermarry; blacks and Africans of mixed race were prohibited from using the same bathrooms as whites; blacks could not own land, were relegated to work only at certain jobs, and needed permission from whites to travel.

16
00:02:26.517 --> 00:02:38.448
Universities were closed to black students, and all blacks had to carry passbooks with their photograph, an identification number, and proof of employment and yearly tax payments.

17
00:02:38.449 --> 00:02:44.725
Black Africans could not vote or have any representation in South Africa’s government.

18
00:02:44.726 --> 00:02:51.834
Mandela first experienced discrimination under apartheid while working in Johannesburg as a lawyer.

19
00:02:51.835 --> 00:02:58.206
Luckily, he had been able to get his degree before the universities were closed to black students.

20
00:02:58.207 --> 00:03:09.798
Mandela quickly became appalled that most of his clients were blacks charged with breaking petty laws, for example, not carrying their passbook when on the street.

21
00:03:09.799 --> 00:03:16.869
Mandela was angry that most laws repressed people of color rather than protected them.

22
00:03:16.870 --> 00:03:34.576
One of these laws was the Bantu Education Act of 1953, which dictated that education for Bantus, as South African blacks were called, would be limited to only what they would need for the sole jobs available to them&#8212;menial jobs.

23
00:03:34.577 --> 00:03:46.328
Since blacks could no longer aspire to be lawyers, teachers, and businesspeople, the government felt there was no point in providing them with more than the most basic education.

24
00:03:46.329 --> 00:04:04.046
Even before the Bantu Education Act, Mandela had already been a member of the African National Congress (ANC)&#8212;an organization dedicated to fighting for independence from white rule through peaceful demonstrations, strikes, and negotiations.

25
00:04:04.047 --> 00:04:14.532
From the time he joined in 1944, Mandela’s leadership skills quickly marked him for a position as an influential member of the group.

26
00:04:14.533 --> 00:04:26.181
In 1952, he was arrested, tried, and given a suspended sentence for initiating the Defiance Campaign against Unjust Laws.

27
00:04:26.182 --> 00:04:46.233
This ANC campaign urged black Africans to passively resist apartheid laws, but in 1960, sixty-seven people who were peacefully demonstrating against the passbook laws were killed by police, in what was infamously known as the Sharpeville Massacre.

28
00:04:46.234 --> 00:04:59.684
In the aftermath, black freedom organizations such as the ANC were outlawed by the government, and Mandela sadly realized peaceful demonstrations would not achieve freedom.

29
00:04:59.685 --> 00:05:15.112
In March 1961, Mandela, who had gone underground, paid a surprise visit to a conference of black South African leaders who were demanding a non-racial, democratic constitution for South Africa.

30
00:05:15.113 --> 00:05:19.436
The country was about to declare itself free from British rule.

31
00:05:19.437 --> 00:05:29.823
At the conference, Mandela warned the government to allow full political participation of all South Africans, or the civil unrest would continue.

32
00:05:29.824 --> 00:05:39.695
But after a whites-only vote in May, South Africa announced itself a republic and continued the repressive apartheid system.

33
00:05:39.696 --> 00:05:50.636
Black leaders decided to demonstrate against the government, now an illegal activity, so Mandela helped form a military wing of the ANC.

34
00:05:50.637 --> 00:06:00.717
Called "Spear of the Nation," this wing of the ANC planned guerilla attacks against South African governmental installations.

35
00:06:00.718 --> 00:06:10.436
In 1962, Mandela left the country illegally to recruit political support from other African countries and nations around the world.

36
00:06:10.437 --> 00:06:17.116
He was charged with leaving the country without permission and sentenced to five years in prison.

37
00:06:17.117 --> 00:06:32.591
While he was in prison, police raided a house in a Johannesburg suburb called Rivonia and claimed they found evidence that Mandela had been involved in treasonous activities to violently overthrow the South African government.

38
00:06:32.592 --> 00:06:40.252
Mandela and eight other defendants were put on trial for their lives in October 1963.

39
00:06:40.253 --> 00:06:52.248
At the opening of the defense portion of the trial, Mandela described the injustices that had brought the ANC to this point and closed his statement with the following words:

40
00:06:52.249 --> 00:06:58.398
"During my lifetime, I have dedicated myself to this struggle of the African people . . .

41
00:06:58.399 --> 00:07:08.996
I have cherished the ideal of a democratic and free society in which all persons live together in harmony and with equal opportunities.

42
00:07:08.997 --> 00:07:13.794
It is an ideal which I hope to live for and to achieve.

43
00:07:13.795 --> 00:07:19.833
But if needs be, it is an ideal for which I am prepared to die."

44
00:07:19.834 --> 00:07:30.488
Because of the immense publicity of the Rivonia Trial, as it came to be known, the eyes of the world slowly turned to the injustices in South Africa.

45
00:07:30.489 --> 00:07:40.981
The United Nations passed a resolution urging amnesty for those imprisoned for opposing apartheid, particularly Mandela and his fellow defendants.

46
00:07:40.982 --> 00:07:48.199
Although they did not receive amnesty, the defendants were sentenced to life in prison rather than to death.

47
00:07:48.200 --> 00:07:56.180
For the next 27 years, Mandela remained imprisoned in a small cell that was bugged with listening devices.

48
00:07:56.181 --> 00:08:05.852
He was constantly monitored, and even when his mother and oldest son were killed in a car crash, he was not allowed to attend their funerals.

49
00:08:05.853 --> 00:08:19.386
His freedom to write letters and receive visitors was severely restricted, but as time went by the world began to listen, resulting in even more political pressure on the South African government.

50
00:08:19.387 --> 00:08:32.210
The anti-apartheid movement exploded across the world, as corporations, universities, churches, and countries refused to invest their money in South Africa in protest.

51
00:08:32.211 --> 00:08:46.964
In 1985, musicians Bruce Springsteen and Miles Davis released a CD called Sun City, and the song "Free Nelson Mandela" reached the top ten on the rock charts in England.

52
00:08:46.965 --> 00:08:59.970
In 1988, on the twenty-fourth year of Mandela’s imprisonment, a rock concert celebrating his seventieth birthday was held outside London and televised in sixty countries.

53
00:08:59.971 --> 00:09:16.060
Very slowly, the policies of the South African government changed, and when F.W. de Klerk became president of South Africa, Nelson Mandela was finally freed to world acclaim on February 10, 1990.

54
00:09:16.061 --> 00:09:35.118
Through Mandela’s tireless work, a new constitution granting all Africans equal rights was signed on November 18, 1993, and on May 10, 1994, he became the first democratically elected president of South Africa.

55
00:09:35.119 --> 00:09:48.545
Since his retirement in 1999, Nelson Mandela has continued working to free oppressed peoples, support human rights, and control the spread of HIV/AIDS in Africa.

56
00:09:48.546 --> 00:10:01.417
For leading his country on what he called "the long walk to freedom," he earned the Nobel Peace Prize in 1993 and the title of the Father of South Africa.

